from ./Admin import Admin
from ./Availability import DomainCheck
from ./Email import Email
from ./Naming import EffortLevels

export anchor Failures:
    meaning:
        Something can fail while a person works: the model that writes
        names, the lookup that checks a domain, the payment provider, or
        the application itself. Every failure gives the person three things
        and nothing else: a plain message, a way to try again, and no
        charge for what failed.
    message:
        The message says what failed in words a person understands, names
        the part that failed, and says what the person can do next. It
        never shows a model name, an error code, a stack trace or the raw
        reply of another service. A technical detail is kept for the
        operator and never shown on the page.
    retry:
        Every failed action offers one retry in the message itself. The
        retry repeats that action only, with the same inputs, and never
        starts a different action or spends a different amount. A person may
        also retry by hand later, and the message stays until it is
        dismissed or replaced by a success.
    noCharge:
        A round, a revision or a check that fails spends nothing from the
        allowance and nothing from the credits. A round that produced no
        names is never counted, as @{EffortLevels} and @{DomainCheck} say.
    partial:
        A partial failure never hides what worked. Names that did arrive
        stay on the list, and a shortlist check keeps every result that
        arrived while the names without an answer stay unknown.
    paymentFailure:
        A failure of the payment provider leaves the plan as it was. The
        person keeps every name on screen and keeps working on the plan they
        already had, and is told plainly that the payment did not go
        through.
    repeatAlert:
        A failure that repeats is reported to the operator. One failure is
        enough to record. The same part failing several times within a short
        window, or several people failing the same part at once, raises an
        alert under @{OperatorAlerts}.
    offline:
        A person who loses the connection sees that the page is out of
        date, keeps their work, and can retry when the connection returns.
        Nothing is shown as saved while the connection is down.
    outage:
        A provider that is slow or down for a long time is shown as the
        failures above, one round at a time, and not as a new state of the
        product. Each round that fails gives its plain message and its one
        retry, and spends nothing. A round is never held in a queue to
        start when the provider returns, so no person waits without a
        message. The repeated failure raises an alert under
        @{OperatorAlerts}, and the level works again by itself when the
        provider answers, with no action needed to restore it. The
        operator may also mark the affected level unavailable by hand, so a
        person sees a plain message instead of a failing attempt.

export anchor OperatorAlerts:
    what:
        An alert is a message to the operator that part of the product is
        failing for real people. It is the only way the operator learns of a
        failure without a person writing to support.
    raises:
        - A model failing for several rounds in a short window
        - A lookup source failing, timing out or rate limiting for many names
        - A payment provider failing or refusing several payments in a row
        - A rise in the page failing to save work
        - A single person failing the same action several times in a row
    where:
        The operator sees alerts in the admin interface under @{Admin},
        newest first, with the part that failed, how many people were
        affected, when it began and the technical detail.
    sends:
        An alert also leaves by email when the same part alerts twice in a
        short window, under @{Email}. An ordinary single failure is not
        emailed.
    actions:
        - Dismiss an alert once the part works again, and the alert returns if it fails again
        - Mark a part unavailable, so a person sees a plain message instead of a failing attempt
        - Read the technical detail of one failure for support
    privacy:
        An alert holds no company description, no generated name and no
        domain result from any person's project. It holds the part that
        failed, the time and the reason.
    limits:
        Alerts are never sold, never shown to another person, and never
        replace the plain message the person gets on the page.
